Z||000000||DXR_DAD||11-05-2000||06:17 PM||||How do they come up with the speed rating<BR>of a tire? One of those things I always<BR>wanted to know.<P><BR>Thanks<BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000001||fropuff||11-05-2000||09:53 PM||||It's the highest speed you can go without the tires blowing out.  Actually, I went on <A HREF="http://www.askjeeves.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.askjeeves.com</A>  and asked Jeeves.  He said that the tire speed rating was the safest top speed that you can go under safe conditions.  That doesn't help too much; that's just what it said.  I have no idea how they get that speed.  I guess professional drivers go as fast as they can until the driver feels unsafe.  That makes a little sense to me.  That's what the odd numbers are for.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||11-05-2000||11:09 PM||||I've been told that the speed rating is the top speed a tire can maintain for a set amount of time without over heating and meltdown happening.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-05-2000||11:11 PM||||I've been digging around on that one.<P>The best place to read about it is the tire maker web sites. You have to dig around allot but it's there.<P>I forget the exact details. It's derived under test conditions that are standardized by USDOT/NHTSA. The tire is at maximum rated inflation at specific loads and lasts a specific amount of time.<P>The speed rating as I understand it is for a max of 15-30 minutes. It is not the speed the tire can be run at all day. Inflation pressure wheel alignment and load will all severely alter the rating.||||reg||2
Z||000004||MrPBody||11-06-2000||07:23 PM||||Tire information: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/2195/tyre_bible.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/2195/tyre_bible.html</A> ||||reg||1
